Beryllium: renderers can enter Capsicum capability mode (opt-in, Phase 2)

Purpose: the first step of the renderer sandbox (plan/14-RENDERER-SANDBOX.md
  increment 2; feature matrix S-REN-01, S-REN-02, S-HRD-03). On FreeBSD
  every renderer already reaches SandboxLinux::InitializeSandbox() through
  RendererMainPlatformDelegate::EnableSandbox(), after PreSandboxInit() and
  before page content and Mojo set-up; the imported FreeBSD code there only
  warms up a few values and lowers RLIMIT_DATA. This patch adds the
  lockdown at that point, for renderers only:
  - warm-up of what the Capsicum survey (freebsd/tests/browser, run
    capsicum --diagnose, owner's machine, 4 November 2026) showed renderers
    read that capability mode refuses: the memory and shared-memory sysctls
    (vm.stats.vm.v_page_count and v_page_size, about 19 reads per renderer;
    kern.ipc.shmmax), uname() (kern.version), the executable path
    (kern.proc.pathname), time zone data (/etc/localtime);
  - base/system on FreeBSD keeps those answers once read: total physical
    memory, kern.ipc.shmmax and uname() never change while the browser
    runs, and their failure paths are NOTREACHED() or CHECK, which would
    end a confined renderer. Available memory, which does change, reports
    0 in capability mode instead of ending the process;
  - a utrace(2) marker ("BERYLLIUM-LOCKDOWN") at the lockdown point, always,
    so the survey can split each renderer's trace into before and after
    the lockdown; with the feature on it is the last call before
    cap_enter(), after the descriptor sweep; utrace is a no-op unless the
    process is traced;
  - cap_enter(), checked with cap_getmode(), only when the feature
    BerylliumCapsicumRenderer is enabled
    (--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer). Daily use is unchanged
    until the lockdown is shown to work. When it is requested and fails,
    the renderer ends (fail closed); when it succeeds, one log line
    "beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N capability_mode=1";
  - with BerylliumCapsicumTrap also enabled, PROC_TRAPCAP_CTL, so the first
    call capability mode refuses ends the renderer with SIGTRAP and a
    backtrace instead of failing quietly (for finding the next warm-up);
  - from Alex S's 2026 Capsicum prototype for the FreeBSD port
    (plan/15-CAPSICUM-PROTOTYPE-EVALUATION.md): caph_cache_tzdata() and
    caph_cache_catpages() in the warm-up, dlopen of /lib/libgcc_s.so.1
    (libthr loads it by path for unwinding when a thread exits), and
    caph_limit_stdio() before cap_enter() (a failure there is logged, not
    fatal).
  - before cap_enter(), every descriptor from 3 up is judged by the type
    the kernel reports for it (kern.proc.filedesc) and cut down to the
    rights that type needs (plan/06 section 3.2; S-REN-04, S-REN-05):
    a read-only regular file with a path (the resource paks, the V8
    snapshot) keeps read, seek, read-only mapping, fstat and F_GETFL; a
    socket (the Mojo channel, the ipcz transports) keeps send, receive,
    event, fstat, shutdown, get/setsockopt and F_GETFL/F_SETFL, never
    connect, bind, listen or accept; a kqueue keeps kevent, event and
    fstat; an eventfd, pipe or fifo keeps read, write, event, fstat,
    F_GETFL/F_SETFL and the ioctl FIONREAD; shared memory keeps what its
    creator gave it less truncation, executable mapping and ownership
    changes; no other ioctl anywhere. The kqueue and eventfd details come
    from epoll-shim, on which Chromium's epoll message pump runs here: its
    epoll_wait() polls the kqueue before each kevent(), and at registration
    it tells pipes from eventfds and kqueues with FIONREAD. In 0.20.3.8,
    which gave kqueues kevent only, every poll failed at once and every
    renderer spun (6 million refusals after the lockdown in the survey of
    7 November 2026, the suite passing all the while). Rights are cut with cap_rights_limit to the intersection of
    what the descriptor has and what the policy allows (the kernel
    refuses to widen rights), fcntls with cap_fcntls_limit the same way,
    ioctls with cap_ioctls_limit to none. A directory is closed and
    counted: capability mode still allows lookups beneath one, and the
    survey of 0.20.3.3 found renderers holding /, /usr, /usr/local and
    /usr/local/etc, inherited from the browser
    (beryllium-close-inherited-descriptors.patch stops the inheritance;
    this is the check behind it). Anything else (a writable or unnamed
    regular file, a device, a terminal, a semaphore, a process
    descriptor...) breaks the policy: the lockdown fails and the renderer
    ends, naming the descriptor. The log line reports
    closed_directories=N limited=N. What arrives later over the Mojo
    channel carries the rights its sender gave it; limiting those before
    they are sent is the browser's part (S-IPC-01, a later increment).
  Later increments make it the default and fail closed in the production
  package (charter D5), limit what the browser passes, and add kernel
  attestation and status reporting.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on the imported FreeBSD patches
  patch-sandbox_policy_freebsd_sandbox__freebsd.cc,
  patch-base_system_sys__info__freebsd.cc and
  patch-base_system_sys__info__posix.cc (stage 2).
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Built and
  run since 0.20.3.3.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/run capsicum --diagnose (the survey splits at
  the marker); with --browser-arg=--enable-features=BerylliumCapsicumRenderer,
  test_50_sandbox shows renderers in capability mode and the rest of the
  suite shows what still breaks. Owner's machine, 0.20.3.3, 4 November
  2026: with the feature, every renderer in capability mode and the whole
  suite passing; the survey's after-lockdown list holds only
  cpuset_getaffinity (18) and one open. 0.20.3.4, 5 November 2026: every
  renderer in capability mode, no directory left to sweep, and the suite
  passing except test_renderers_hold_no_directories_or_writable_files
  (shared-memory files; beryllium-anonymous-shared-memory.patch); no open
  after the lockdown any more (probably the libgcc_s preload); the 18
  cpuset_getaffinity calls are most likely libc's
  sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN), which asks the root cpuset first (refused
  in capability mode) and falls back to the processor count from the ELF
  auxiliary vector. The marker then came before the descriptor sweep,
  whose kern.proc.filedesc reads (2 per renderer) the survey counted as
  after; it now comes after. 0.20.3.7, 5 November 2026: the whole suite
  passes with and without the lockdown, and no renderer holds a directory
  or writable file. The per-descriptor policy (build 0.20.3.8) is checked
  from outside by test_50_sandbox
  test_confined_renderer_descriptors_follow_the_policy (procstat -f and
  -C against the same policy) and test_45_fonts (the same platform fonts
  in a confined renderer as in an unconfined one). 0.20.3.8, 7 November
  2026: the suite passes with and without the lockdown and the fonts test
  passes; the survey shows the kqueue poll loop, fixed for 0.20.3.9
  (kqueue: CAP_EVENT; eventfd and pipe: FIONREAD), which also gains
  test_90_stability test_renderers_idle_without_spinning.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, process confinement)

